      "text": "To solve for \\(h\\), the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\), we will use the Pythagorean theorem, which states that \\(h = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}\\).\n\nFirst, we need to express the logarithms in terms of base 2. Recall the change of base formula: \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\).\n\n1. Convert \\(\\log_4 27\\) to base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\n\n2. The angle \\(\\log_2 9\\) is already directly in terms of base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 \n\\]\n\nNext, we compute these values in decimal form:\n\\[\n\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_2 3 \\approx 1.585\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 27 \\approx 3 \\times 1.585 = 4.755\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these values into the expression for \\(h\\):\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\right)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculate each square:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\log_2 9)^2 = (\\log_2 (3^2))^2 = (2 \\log_2 3)^2 = 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\nEvaluate the sum:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\right)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\nFactor out \\((\\log_2 3)^2\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2 = (\\log_2 3)^2 \\left(\\frac{9}{4} + 4\\right) = (\\log_2 3)^2 \\left(\\frac{9 + 16}{4} \\right) = (\\log_2 3)^2 \\left(\\frac{25}{4} \\right)\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)(\\log_2 3)^2} = \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we compute \\(4^h\\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5 = 243\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}\n\\]",
